Problems

1. Very weak validation  
   • `[^\s]+` means “anything except a white-space”, so the function will accept  
     http://☠/<script>alert(1)</script> or http:// as “valid”.  
   • International‐Domain-Name, IPv6, port, path, query, and fragment rules are
     not checked.  
   • Upper-case schemes (HTTP://) will be rejected.  

2. Possible security issues if the result is used for access control, open
   redirects, XSS filtering, etc.; an attacker can craft a string that passes
   the test but is not a legal URL or points somewhere you did not intend.

3. Wrong tool for the job  
   A custom regex is error-prone; JavaScript already has URL parsing support
   (WHATWG URL in all modern browsers and Node ≥ 10).

4. Performance / style  
   • The RegExp object is rebuilt on every call instead of being cached.  
   • No type check: non-string input will throw.  
   • Unnecessary use of the RegExp constructor—`/…/` literal is clearer.

Fix

Use the built-in URL constructor and verify the parts you care about:

```javascript
// One-time compiled helper (can be placed in a util module)
export function isValidHttpUrl(value) {
  if (typeof value !== 'string') return false;

  try {
    const url = new URL(value);
    // Accept only http or https
    return url.protocol === 'http:' || url.protocol === 'https:';
  } catch {
    return false;          // URL constructor threw => not a valid URL
  }
}
```

If you really must use a regex, at least tighten it up and keep it outside
the function:

```javascript
const HTTP_URL_RE = /^(https?):\/\/([a-z0-9-]+(\.[a-z0-9-]+)+|localhost)(:\d{1,5})?(\/[^\s]*)?$/i;

function isValidURL(str) {
  return typeof str === 'string' && HTTP_URL_RE.test(str);
}
```

Additional recommendations

• Document exactly what “valid” means (schema only? host? path?).  
• Reject dangerous schemes (`javascript:`, `data:`, etc.) explicitly if the URL
  is later embedded in HTML.  
• Add unit tests that cover edge cases: punycode, IPv6, port numbers, spaces,
  etc.
